  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1120 AM MDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nTHUNDERSTORMS THIS AFTERNOON/EVENING: \n\nA shortwave trough will move across the region today and \ntonight. This will provide enhanced lift and support scattered \nto widespread convection as it moves through. It will also help \nspeed up storm motions compared to previous days. Despite this, \nlocalized flash flooding remains on the table if stronger and/or\ntraining storms move over steep terrain and recent burn scars. \nSee the Hydrology section below for more information. \n\nLATE WEEK PATTERN CHANGE: \n\nBehind the aformentioned shortwave, dry air begins to filter \nback into the region. It will take a few days before residual \nmoisture is completely scrubbed from the atmosphere, so we \nexpect some convection Thursday and Friday with coverage \ndecreasing each day. As things dry out, the primary threat of \nthunderstorms will switch from heavy rainfall to gusty outflow \nwinds up to 50 mph. \n\nHigh pressure sets up over Utah this weekend bringing hot and \nmostly dry conditions. Temperatures will run around 10 degrees \nabove normal, but with less moisture in the air, you can expect \nmore of a \"dry heat\" compared to what we've endured lately.",
